 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookup is served from a local embedded SQLite index file on the kiosk. Each kiosk builds its own index from the snapshot it imports; no index is shipped inside a snapshot and no service runs alongside the kiosk application.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the index for the new snapshot in a staging file separate from the active index, so partially imported records are never visible to lookups. Activate the staged index only after signature, schema, uniqueness, and index build have all succeeded. An import failure discards the staging file, leaves the previous snapshot and its index active, and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Look up the normalized serial by exact match against the active index. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, built on the kiosk at import | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Lookup must complete from files on the kiosk with no runtime network access, and kiosks have no network route in normal operation. The HTTPS directory and managed search service both put registry lookups behind a network dependency (plus timeout handling, credentials, and recurring cost for the managed service). SQLite gives predictable lookups with no service to run; the cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives, which stays inside this node. |
